description.narrative:
CAFOD Kenya and Uganda Programme launched a new programme strategy in 2023. The 2023-2030 KU strategy aspires, alongside our partners, to contribute towards resilient communities where people, particularly women, girls, and other marginalized people, thrive in a peaceful, well-governed, and healthy, supportive natural environment. Kenya-Uganda programme strategy seeks to address four key drivers of social and environmental vulnerability in fragile environments in the northern regions of Kenya and Uganda: environmental degradation, climate shocks, poor governance, and conflict.
To actualize these aspirations, the overall objective of the Needs Assessment is to assess the current situation, identify the gaps and needs of the targeted communities and recommend key interventions that meet the real needs of the targeted communities in Marsabit, Isiolo, and Samburu County and at national level for policy and advocacy across the sectors below in line with the new programme strategy. The following thematic areas among others will be addressed; Food and nutrition security, Resilience building and Livelihoods, WASH, Natural resource management, Women's Economic Empowerment, Disaster Risk Reduction and Climate Change Adaptation, Peacebuilding and good governance, Capacity Strengthening;

The findings of the needs assessment will inform the development of CAFOD and institutional-funded Programmes. By identifying and analyzing the needs across different sectors, CAFOD and Partners will develop a better understanding of the sectors that require more focus. By considering multiple sectors together, a more holistic and integrated approach and or develop specific projects, will be adopted to meet the needs of communities and populations effectively and ensure these are aligned to programme strategy.
